course: everyday
lesson: 409
title: Lag, crashes and blaming the wifi
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Leo was one hit away from winning. His finger was on the button. Then the screen froze. When it came back, Leo was lying on the floor of the game and the other team was dancing. He shouted «THE INTERNET ROBBED ME!» His dad went detective and found the culprit in the next room: Leo's sister, streaming a film in high quality. A house shares one connection, like a family sharing one bathroom, and when everyone uses it at once, someone waits — that waiting is lag.
explain: Kai shouted «LAG!» every time he lost, even when the game ran as smooth as butter. The squad stopped believing him. Then one evening he really did have lag, his character walking into a wall for ten seconds, and nobody believed a word. The boy who cried lag. Jayden's team had the opposite problem: after a loss they all blamed Jayden's lag, until they checked his ping — the number that shows how fast the connection is — and it was 20, which is low and perfectly fine. The other team was just better.
explain: Theo decided he was a tech genius and turned the router off and on forty times in one evening. Each time, the whole house lost the internet: his mum's video call, his sister's homework, the smart speaker in the kitchen. His dad gently took the router away. Doing the same fix over and over rarely fixes anything; when the connection keeps failing, ask a grown-up.
explain: Priya's game crashed and an hour of Minecraft building vanished, so now she saves often and sets autosave to every five minutes. Noah's tablet got so hot from a long game that it switched itself off; it was protecting itself, and his dad said tablet and boy both needed a break. Ruby went to her grandma's in the countryside, where the game would not even load, so she played cards with grandma instead and discovered grandma had the best poker face in the county.
explain: Families found fixes together. Ollie's mum turns the wifi off at 8pm, and his squad thought he kept rage-quitting, until he told them about the 8pm rule. Zara plays on an old, slow laptop, so her squad picked games that run fine on old computers. And when Mia's sister kept downloading huge updates in the middle of Mia's matches, the arguing stopped once the two of them agreed set times for big downloads.
fact: Lag is a delay between what you press and what happens in an online game.
fact: All the devices in a house usually share one internet connection.
fact: Films, video calls and big downloads on the same connection can cause lag in games.
fact: Ping is a number showing how long data takes to travel; lower ping means less lag.
fact: Switching a router off and on cuts the internet for everyone in the house.
fact: Saving often protects your work if a game crashes.
fact: Devices can switch themselves off when they get too hot, to protect themselves.
fact: The countryside often has slower internet than towns and cities.
fact: Blaming lag every time makes people stop believing you when it is true.
fact: Telling your team about your family's internet rules helps them understand when you go offline.
exercise: Why did Leo's game freeze just before he won? | His sister was streaming a film on the same connection | The other team found his address and switched his internet off | His controller ran out of battery | The game made him lose on purpose
exercise: How did Leo feel when the game came back and he had lost? | Robbed by the internet itself | Pleased that the long match was finally over | Calm, because it was only a bit of lag | Proud of the other team for winning
exercise: What is lag? | A delay between pressing and seeing it happen | A kind of cheat that slows down the other players | A rank between silver and gold | A crash that deletes your save
exercise: Why did the squad stop believing Kai about lag? | He shouted «LAG!» even when the game was smooth | He never had any lag at all, not even once | His internet was the fastest in town | He told them he was cheating
exercise: What does a low ping mean? | Less lag and a faster connection | More lag and a slower, jumpier connection | A quieter game sound | A lower rank in the game
exercise: What showed that Jayden's lag was not to blame? | His ping was perfectly fine | His sister was not at home that night | He had the newest and fastest console | His team won the next match easily
exercise: What happened when Theo turned the router off and on forty times? | The whole house kept losing the internet | The game became twice as fast as before | Only Theo's game reset each time | Nothing, it fixed everything
exercise: What should you do when the connection keeps failing? | Ask a grown-up to look at it | Keep switching the router off and on until it works | Hit the router to wake it up | Blame your teammates
exercise: How did Priya stop losing her building to crashes? | She saves often, with autosave every five minutes | She never builds anything big any more, just huts | She plays only in the morning | She turns the router off first
exercise: Why did Noah's tablet switch itself off? | It got too hot and protected itself | It ran out of games to play | Noah's mum switched it off from her phone | The game was finished
exercise: What was wise for Noah when the tablet overheated? | Let it cool down and take a break himself | Put it in the freezer at once to cool it fast | Keep playing as soon as it switched on | Cover it with a blanket
exercise: What did Ruby do at grandma's when the game would not load? | Played cards with grandma | Cried and sulked all afternoon | Walked into town to find wifi | Called her squad to complain
exercise: Ollie's mum turns off the wifi at 8pm and the squad thinks he rage-quit. What helped? | He told them about the 8pm rule | He rage-quit for real the next night to prove a point | He hid the rule from them | He stopped playing for ever
exercise: Zara plays on an old slow laptop. What did her squad do? | Picked games that run fine on old computers | Played without her and didn't tell her | Told her to buy a new one with her birthday money | Laughed at her laptop
exercise: Mia's sister downloads a huge update mid-match. What stopped the arguing? | A deal about times for big downloads | Mia unplugging the router in the middle of it | Hiding her sister's tablet | Shouting through the wall
exercise: Why is a house's internet like a shared bathroom? | When everyone uses it at once, someone waits | It only works in the morning before school | Only one person may ever use it | It is cleaned once a week
test: «LAG!!» Kai screams, but his game's smooth as butter. What'll happen next time it's real? | Nobody will believe him | Everyone will rush to help | The game will give him the win | His ping will drop to zero
test: «my game keeps freezing at 7, every night». Possible reason? | Others at home streaming or calling at that time | The game servers get tired every evening at seven | The controller gets tired | The moon blocks the wifi
test: Theo: «just unplug it again, 41st time lucky». Better idea? | Stop and ask a grown-up to check it | Unplug it faster this time | Unplug the neighbour's too | Shake the router hard
test: «we lost cos of Jayden's lag». Jayden's ping says 20. So? | The lag wasn't the reason they lost | Jayden must be lying about his ping | 20 means terrible lag | The game is broken
test: crash, an hour of building gone. Priya: «never again». What does she do? | Saves often and sets autosave | Never builds anything big again | Builds only on Sunday mornings | Unplugs the router before playing
test: tablet's boiling hot and just switched off. Noah: «did I break it??» | Probably not, it protected itself; let it cool | Yes, it's ruined for good, so throw it away | Put it in the fridge | Plug it in and play straight away
test: at nan's, no signal, game won't load. «worst day ever». Good move? | Find another kind of fun, like cards with nan | Sulk until you go home | Climb on the roof to find a better signal | Make nan pay for faster internet at her house
test: squad: «Ollie rage-quit AGAIN». Ollie: «nah, mum switches wifi off at 8». What should he do? | Tell them the 8pm rule so they understand | Say nothing and let them think he rage-quit | Hide the router from mum | Quit the squad
test: «my sister's downloading a massive update, this is sabotage». Fair fix? | Agree times for big downloads at home | Delete her update while she isn't looking | Unplug her tablet | Download something bigger back
test: «old laptop, everything's slow, guess I can't play with them». Good friends would? | Pick games that run fine on her laptop | Tell her to stay home until she gets a new one | Play without telling her | Make fun of the laptop
exam: Identify the most probable cause of intermittent delay in an online game where other members of the household are simultaneously streaming video. | The shared household connection is being used by several devices concurrently | The game operator is deliberately impeding the participant on account of his rank | The controller has ceased to transmit signals | The participant's rank has been reduced
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the repeated disconnection of the household router in an attempt to remedy delay? | It deprives every member of the household of the connection and rarely resolves the fault | It invariably restores the connection to full speed within a minute of each disconnection | It affects the participant's device exclusively | It is the remedy recommended by all operators
exam: State the precaution most consistent with established practice for preventing the loss of work in the event of a game ceasing to function. | The work ought to be saved frequently, with automatic saving enabled | The device ought to be kept in continuous operation | The router ought to be disconnected prior to play | Construction ought to be confined to a single session
exam: Whereas a tablet has switched itself off on account of excessive heat, select the response consistent with the established facts. | The device ought to be permitted to cool, and the user to rest | The device ought to be placed forthwith in a freezer | The device ought to be restarted and play resumed immediately | The device ought to be covered so as to retain warmth
